            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Publishing Your Application</h2>
                    <p>Publishing prepares your application for deployment. .NET offers several publishing options depending on your needs—from simple deployments to optimized single-file executables.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Framework-Dependent vs Self-Contained</h2>
                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Framework-Dependent</h4>
                            <p>Requires .NET runtime to be installed on the target machine.</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>✅ Smaller file size</li>
                                <li>✅ Shares runtime with other apps</li>
                                <li>❌ Runtime must be pre-installed</li>
                            </ul>
                            <code>dotnet publish</code>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Self-Contained</h4>
                            <p>Includes the .NET runtime with your app.</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>✅ No runtime installation needed</li>
                                <li>✅ Control exact runtime version</li>
                                <li>❌ Larger file size (~60-80MB)</li>
                            </ul>
                            <code>dotnet publish --self-contained</code>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Basic Publishing</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Framework-dependent (requires runtime on target)
dotnet publish -c Release

# Self-contained (includes runtime)
dotnet publish -c Release --self-contained

# Output is in bin/Release/net8.0/publish/</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Publishing for Specific Platforms</h2>
                    <p>Use Runtime Identifiers (RIDs) to target specific platforms:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Windows x64
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained

# Windows ARM64
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-arm64 --self-contained

# Linux x64
dotnet publish -c Release -r linux-x64 --self-contained

# macOS Apple Silicon
dotnet publish -c Release -r osx-arm64 --self-contained

# macOS Intel
dotnet publish -c Release -r osx-x64 --self-contained</code></pre>

                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Common RIDs</span>
                        </div>
                        <p><code>win-x64</code>, <code>win-arm64</code>, <code>linux-x64</code>, <code>linux-arm64</code>, <code>osx-x64</code>, <code>osx-arm64</code>, <code>linux-musl-x64</code> (Alpine)</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Single File Deployment</h2>
                    <p>Bundle everything into a single executable:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Single file, self-contained
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained \
    -p:PublishSingleFile=true

# Even smaller with trimming
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained \
    -p:PublishSingleFile=true \
    -p:PublishTrimmed=true</code></pre>

                    <p>Or configure in the project file:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;PublishSingleFile&gt;true&lt;/PublishSingleFile&gt;
  &lt;SelfContained&gt;true&lt;/SelfContained&gt;
  &lt;RuntimeIdentifier&gt;win-x64&lt;/RuntimeIdentifier&gt;
  &lt;PublishTrimmed&gt;true&lt;/PublishTrimmed&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>ReadyToRun (R2R) Compilation</h2>
                    <p>Pre-compile to native code for faster startup:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained \
    -p:PublishReadyToRun=true</code></pre>

                    <p>This increases file size but reduces JIT compilation at startup.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Native AOT Compilation</h2>
                    <p>.NET 8+ supports Ahead-of-Time compilation to native code:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;PublishAot&gt;true&lt;/PublishAot&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64</code></pre>

                    <p>Benefits:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Fastest startup time</li>
                        <li>Smallest memory footprint</li>
                        <li>No JIT compilation</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="warning-box">
                        <div class="warning-box-header">
                            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
                                <path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"/>
                                <line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"/>
                            </svg>
                            <span>Limitations</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>Native AOT doesn't support all .NET features (like runtime code generation). Test thoroughly before using in production.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Docker Deployment</h2>
                    <p>Containerize your .NET application:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-dockerfile"># Dockerfile
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk:8.0 AS build
WORKDIR /src

# Copy and restore
COPY *.csproj ./
RUN dotnet restore

# Copy and build
COPY . ./
RUN dotnet publish -c Release -o /app/publish

# Runtime image
FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/publish .
EXPOSE 8080
ENTRYPOINT ["dotnet", "MyApp.dll"]</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Build the image
docker build -t myapp .

# Run the container
docker run -p 8080:8080 myapp</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Deploying to Azure</h2>
                    
                    <h3>Azure App Service</h3>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Login to Azure
az login

# Create resource group
az group create --name myResourceGroup --location eastus

# Create App Service plan
az appservice plan create --name myPlan --resource-group myResourceGroup --sku B1

# Create web app
az webapp create --name myapp --resource-group myResourceGroup --plan myPlan

# Deploy
dotnet publish -c Release -o ./publish
cd publish
zip -r ../deploy.zip .
az webapp deploy --resource-group myResourceGroup --name myapp --src-path ../deploy.zip</code></pre>

                    <h3>Using Visual Studio / VS Code</h3>
                    <p>Right-click the project and select "Publish" to use the GUI-based deployment wizard.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Deploying to Linux</h2>
                    <p>For a Linux server:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Publish for Linux
dotnet publish -c Release -r linux-x64 --self-contained -o ./publish

# Copy to server
scp -r ./publish/* user@server:/var/www/myapp/

# On the server, create a systemd service
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-ini">[Unit]
Description=My .NET App

[Service]
WorkingDirectory=/var/www/myapp
ExecStart=/var/www/myapp/MyApp
Restart=always
RestartSec=10
User=www-data
Environment=ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production
Environment=DOTNET_PRINT_TELEMETRY_MESSAGE=false

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Enable and start
sudo systemctl enable myapp
sudo systemctl start myapp
sudo systemctl status myapp</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Publishing Best Practices</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Always use Release configuration</strong> for production</li>
                        <li><strong>Use self-contained</strong> when you can't control the target environment</li>
                        <li><strong>Enable trimming</strong> for smaller deployments (test thoroughly)</li>
                        <li><strong>Use Docker</strong> for consistent deployments across environments</li>
                        <li><strong>Set environment variables</strong> for configuration (not appsettings.json)</li>
                        <li><strong>Never deploy debug builds</strong> to production</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>dotnet publish -c Release</code> for production deployments.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Choose between framework-dependent and self-contained based on your needs.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use Docker for consistent, reproducible deployments.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Consider Native AOT for the best startup performance.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>

